  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1230 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nCurrently monitoring Tropical System Edouard as it is progged to\nlift northwest across eastern Texas tonight through Thursday.\nDuring the day Wednesday, locally higher wind gusts of 15-20 mph\ncould reach into southeast Oklahoma as the remnants of the\nTropical system approach the Red River. These locally higher wind\ngusts could also tick up fire weather concerns across parts of \nsoutheast Oklahoma Wednesday afternoon. A limiting factor will be\nthe increase in moisture with dewpoints in the lower 70s forecast\nin southeast Oklahoma Wednesday afternoon. \n\nLow chances for showers develop Wednesday night into Thursday for\nmainly far southeast Oklahoma with the remnants of the Tropical\nsystem near the Red River. Overall rainfall amounts are not\ncurrently expected to be meaningful with ongoing drought\nconditions likely to continue. Across the rest of eastern Oklahoma\nand northwest Arkansas, hot and humid conditions continue through\nThursday. Overall, afternoon temperatures each day look to be a\ndegree or two cooler than Monday, though with the increase in\nmoisture the humidity and heat stress should continue. Thus, a\nHeat Advisory has been issued for much of eastern Oklahoma and\nalso the Arkansas River valley of west central Arkansas Wednesday\nafternoon. There is potential for additional heat headlines\nThursday into the weekend as well. \n\nFriday into early next week, the ridge of high pressure is progged\nto become centered back to the west with the CWA on the eastern\nperiphery of the ridge late weekend into next Monday. There are\nhints within the extended data of a weak surface boundary trying\nto backdoor into the eastern portion of the CWA during this time,\nwhich could create a low potential for a diurnal shower/storm or \ntwo within the terrain of western Arkansas. Confidence is low at \nthis time as the ridge remains the dominate feature over the \nsouthern Plains. Either way heat and heat stress remain through \nthe upcoming holiday weekend with likely impacts to outdoor \nevents.",
